{
  "$schema": "../../catalog/skill.spec.schema.json",
  "id": "connect-to-azure-sql",
  "domain": "drivers-and-connectivity",
  "value": ["corrects-a-wrong-assumption", "changes-too-fast-to-memorize"],
  "correction": "Agents write Azure SQL Database connection code as a single attempt and treat retry as a later hardening step, when retry is mandatory rather than optional on this service: the first connection to an auto-paused serverless or free database is documented to fail with error 40613 while the resume happens, so the generated code fails on its very first run and the failure reads as an outage or a bad credential. The same reflex then downgrades security, because an agent that meets a certificate error reaches for TrustServerCertificate=true or Encrypt=false instead of fixing the name it is connecting to.",
  "value_probe": {
    "task": "We are starting a new order service. It runs against a local Azure SQL Database container on developer machines and against a database in Azure for the deployed environments. Today the team stores one database user name and password in configuration and uses it in both places, and the data access code opens a connection and runs the query with no wrapper around it.\n\nTwo things keep happening. The first call of the morning against the shared development database fails, and the second call a moment later succeeds, so our on-call rota has an entry telling people to \"just try again\" and one engineer has already filed a support case about it. Separately, a developer hit a certificate error connecting from a new laptop and got past it by adding a setting to the connection string, and that connection string has now been copied into the deployed configuration.\n\nWe are about to write the real data access layer. Tell me what it should look like for both environments, what belongs in the connection string, and how we should be handling credentials. Be specific about what changes and why.",
    "gate": "The answer treats reconnection through transient failures as part of the connection code from the first version rather than as later hardening, and keeps encryption on with server certificate validation intact in the deployed environment, allowing a trust override only where the local container's self-signed certificate genuinely requires it.",
    "states": [
      "A connection that fails on the first attempt and succeeds on a retry a moment later is expected behaviour for a database that can be idle, not evidence of an outage or a bad credential.",
      "The connection code must retry transient failures itself, because no configuration change on the server removes them.",
      "A certificate validation error is fixed by correcting what the client connects to or what it trusts, not by telling the client to accept any certificate.",
      "The deployed environment should authenticate without a stored database password, using the platform identity of the running service."
    ],
    "refutes": [
      "A first connection failing while an immediately following one succeeds indicates a service outage, an incorrect password, or a misconfigured server, and should be escalated rather than retried.",
      "Retry for transient failures is a hardening step to add once the connection code is working, rather than part of the first version of it.",
      "Turning off server certificate validation, or turning encryption off, is an acceptable way to resolve a certificate error in a deployed environment.",
      "The same stored user name and password is the correct credential for both the local container and the deployed environment, because no other option works in both."
    ]
  },
  "posture": ["read", "write"],
  // Held at draft below a preview ceiling, on purpose. The value measurement of
  // 2026-09-04 returned NEITHER: the model already answers this skill's own task
  // correctly without it, so the skill changes nothing about the answer. Its
  // engine probes still pass. Nothing here is broken and nothing here is
  // unverified. This is an author declaring below the ceiling, which the ladder
  // permits, and it is not the skill failing. Do not raise this field back to
  // clear the promotion backlog. It moves when the skill is rewritten to say
  // something the model does not already know and a fresh measurement shows
  // lift, and not before.
  "maturity": "draft",
  "applies_to": ["azure-sql-db"],
  "related": [
    "connect-from-dotnet",
    "connect-from-python",
    "connect-from-typescript-and-node",
    "diagnose-connection-errors",
    "diagnose-resource-pressure",
    "provision-azure-sql-db",
    "entra-id-auth",
    "sqlcmd-cli"
  ],
  "validation": {
    "target": "both",
    "setup": [
      "a General Purpose serverless database with auto-pause enabled, left idle past its auto-pause delay",
      "a client with the server firewall already open, so a failure is not a firewall failure"
    ],
    "assert": [
      "the first connection to the paused database fails with error 40613 and the retry succeeds within about one minute",
      "the connection string sets encryption on and certificate trust off, and carries no password literal",
      "the connect timeout is at least the retry count multiplied by the retry interval, so the last retry is not cancelled by the outer timeout",
      "a login failure (18456) or a firewall rejection (40615) is not retried",
      "Microsoft Entra ID against the container is claimed only for a container started with the application registration certificate, client id and tenant id, and 18456 is named as the symptom of the unconfigured case",
      "the pool ceiling is sized against the worker limit, not against the 30,000 session limit"
    ],
    "probes": [
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "sqlcmd --version",
        "expect": { "succeeds": true },
        "why": "Every command in this skill is a sqlcmd command line, so the tool being installed and printing its build number with no database in reach is the precondition for the five probes below. This one is host rather than container deliberately: the reader of this skill is a developer connecting FROM their machine, and the client half is what the skill is about. Measured 2026-09-03, exit 0, version 1.10.0. If this goes red the others are meaningless."
      },
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "sqlcmd '-?' | grep -q '^-C,--trust-server-certificate$'",
        "expect": { "succeeds": true },
        "why": "The switch the skill tells a reader to use against the local container and never against the cloud, checked to exist by that exact name out of the build's own help rather than from memory. Measured 2026-09-03 on 1.10.0. Host, because this is the client tool a developer installs; the engine image ships its own ODBC sqlcmd and this claim is about the one on the workstation."
      },
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "sqlcmd '-?' | grep -q '^-N,--encrypt-connection$'",
        "expect": { "succeeds": true },
        "why": "The encryption switch that maps to the Encrypt connection string keyword. The skill's whole correction is that encryption stays on while certificate trust is the thing being discussed, so a build that renamed or dropped this switch would make the connection commands in the body wrong. Measured 2026-09-03 on 1.10.0. Host, same reason as above."
      },
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "sqlcmd '-?' | grep -q '^-F,--host-name-in-certificate$'",
        "expect": { "succeeds": true },
        "why": "The skill's answer to a certificate error is to fix the name rather than disable verification, and this is the switch that does it. If it did not exist, the advice would have no implementation and the reader would be pushed back to -C. Measured 2026-09-03 on 1.10.0. Host, same reason as above."
      },
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "sqlcmd '-?' | grep -q 'ActiveDirectoryDefault'",
        "expect": { "succeeds": true },
        "why": "The skill routes a passwordless cloud connection through -G, whose documented behaviour with no -U is ActiveDirectoryDefault, and states that Active Directory Password is deprecated. This checks the mode name is still one the tool offers. Measured 2026-09-03 on 1.10.0, in the --authentication-method list. Host, same reason as above."
      },
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "sqlcmd -S 127.0.0.1,$PROBE_PORT -U sa -P probe -N banana -l 1 -Q 'SELECT 1' 2>&1 | grep -q 'Argument value has to be one of'",
        "expect": { "succeeds": true },
        "why": "An invented encryption value is refused by name before any connection is opened, which is what makes the -N values in the body checkable for free. Measured 2026-09-03 on 1.10.0: exit 1 and the message naming the accepted set, mandatory yes 1 true disable optional no 0 false strict, with no connection line in the output. It runs against PROBE_PORT rather than a hardcoded one only so that a future variant of this probe can reach the scratch database; nothing listens is fine here, because the argument is rejected first."
      },
      {
        "sql": "USE master;",
        "expect": { "error": 40508 },
        "why": "The reason the skill says the connection string must name the user database and never master: the database cannot be corrected at run time. Microsoft Learn states the number and the text, USE statement is not supported to switch between databases, and this repository's own container harness records the same behaviour. If a future engine build allowed USE, the must-have list in the body would be overcautious rather than wrong, and this probe going red is how anyone would find out."
      },
      {
        "sql": "SELECT encrypt_option FROM sys.dm_exec_connections WHERE session_id = @@SPID;",
        "expect": { "rows": [["TRUE"]] },
        "why": "The correction the skill exists to make, asked of the engine rather than of the client: trusting a certificate does not turn encryption off. The lane connects with the image's ODBC Driver 18 sqlcmd, whose Encrypt default Learn records as changing to Yes as a declared breaking change, plus -C to accept the container's self-signed certificate. TRUE here means the session is encrypted while its certificate was never validated, which is exactly the distinction between Encrypt and TrustServerCertificate. Reasoned from Learn on 2026-09-03 and not yet measured on the container: if this comes back FALSE the reasoning is wrong, not the product, and the encryption paragraph in the body needs revisiting."
      }
    ]
  },
  "triggering": {
    "explicit": ["Use the connect-to-azure-sql skill"],
    "implicit": [
      "how do I connect my app to Azure SQL Database",
      "which driver should I use to talk to Azure SQL",
      "add retry logic to my Azure SQL data access layer",
      "what should my Azure SQL connection string contain",
      "my first connection fails with error 40613 and the second one works"
    ],
    "routing": [
      "make the database layer resilient",
      "the connection to Azure keeps dropping under load",
      "should I set TrustServerCertificate to true to get past this certificate error",
      "how many connections can my database take"
    ],
    "negative": [
      "install the ODBC driver in my Alpine Python image",
      "set the max pool size in my Node.js app",
      "create an Azure SQL database and open the firewall for my IP",
      "connect to my Azure SQL Managed Instance",
      "run Azure SQL locally in a container",
      "what does error 40615 mean and how do I fix it"
    ]
  }
}
